.TH ALESS 1 "2026-11-02" "aless 0.6.0" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
aless \- JSON, YAML, TOML, CSV, XML, INI, Markdown and more as one tree, in a
jless\-style terminal viewer
.SH SYNOPSIS
aless [OPTIONS] [FILE]...
.br
aless [OPTIONS] DIRECTORY
.br
<command> | aless [OPTIONS]
.PP
\-h prints a summary of the options, and \-\-help this whole reference.
.SH DESCRIPTION
aless reads JSON, JSON Lines, JSON5, JSONC, jsonic, YAML, TOML, INI, CSV, TSV,
XML, ZON, Markdown, RSS or Atom feeds, CSS, Protocol Buffers\(aq .proto files,
PGN chess games, C source, grammars in ABNF, EBNF or GBNF, expressions and
semantic versions, and any line\-oriented format an ABNF grammar describes, as
one tree, and shows it in a jless\-style viewer in the terminal, with tabs,
search, a source view and a watch mode that reloads a changed file and keeps
your place.
.SS "The viewer"
aless is a viewer: it draws on a terminal and reads keys from it, and does
nothing else. Each FILE opens in a tab of its own, watched and reloaded when it
changes, keeping your place; a directory opens in the file explorer, where
Enter opens a file; and without a FILE, aless reads standard input when it is
not a terminal, and otherwise explores the current directory. A file that does
not parse opens as a tab showing the parser\(aqs report, with the line and
column it stopped at, and the tab reloads when the file is fixed.
.PP
Without a terminal to draw on and read keys from (standard output a pipe or a
file, no terminal to open, or TERM=dumb) aless refuses to start, at once and
before reading any input, with a line on standard error and the status 2.
Nothing but \-h, \-\-help, \-V and \-\-generate writes to standard output. To
read one of these formats in a script, or write a document as another format,
use dmog (https://github.com/tabnas/dmog), which translates any format aless
reads into any other.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Input options"
.TP 7
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-kind\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fIFORMAT\fR
Parse every input as FORMAT instead of by its extension: a format\(aqs name
(json, jsonl, jsonic, jsonc, json5, yaml, toml, ini, csv, tsv, xml, zon,
markdown, feed, css, proto, pgn, c, abnf, ebnf, gbnf, expr, semver or text),
one of its extensions (yml, md, ndjson), or a \-\-grammar NAME. Standard input
is read as JSON unless this says otherwise. \-\-format is another name for this
option.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-grammar\fR \fINAME=FILE\fR
Read a file whose extension or whole name is NAME, in any case (x.hosts,
/etc/hosts), with the ABNF grammar in FILE, which is read within \-\-max\-size
and compiled within \-\-timeout before any input is read. NAME is then a
format, as \-k and :format take it and the status bar shows it; NAME,NAME2=FILE
gives it two names, and the option repeats. See CUSTOM GRAMMARS.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-grammar\-expr\fR \fINAME=ABNF\fR
\-\-grammar, with the grammar\(aqs text on the command line: everything after
the first =.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-max\-size\fR \fISIZE\fR
Refuse an input larger than SIZE (default 64M; 0 for no limit), and a
\-\-grammar file too: a parse takes about 40 bytes of memory per byte of input.
A file over the limit opens as a tab that says so, and names the size that
would read it; a grammar file over it is a usage error. SIZE is bytes, or a
whole number with K, M or G (1024s).
.TP 7
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR
Stop a parse, or a \-\-grammar compile, that runs longer than SECONDS (2.5,
90s, 2m; default none; 0 for no limit). A parse stopped opens as a tab that
says how far it got; a compile stopped is a usage error. A parse runs at about
a megabyte a second. See LARGE INPUTS AND LIMITS.
.SS "Viewer options"
.TP 7
\fB\-\-no\-watch\fR
Do not reload a file when it changes.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-watch\fR
Reload a file when it changes, keeping your place (the default).
.TP 7
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-mode\fR \fIMODE\fR
Start in data mode (the default), the streamlined tree, or in line mode, every
line of a pretty\-printed rendering; m switches between them.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIN\fR
Fold the containers deeper than N levels when a file opens (default: every
container open); :depth N does the same inside aless.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-indent\fR \fIN\fR
Indent the tree N spaces a level (default 2; at most 16); < and > change it
inside aless.
.TP 7
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-line\-numbers\fR
Show absolute line numbers.
.TP 7
\fB\-N\fR, \fB\-\-no\-line\-numbers\fR
Hide absolute line numbers.
.TP 7
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-relative\-line\-numbers\fR
Show line numbers relative to the focused row.
.TP 7
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-no\-relative\-line\-numbers\fR
Hide relative line numbers.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-scrolloff\fR \fIN\fR
Keep N rows around the focus when scrolling (default 3).
.TP 7
\fB\-\-hidden\fR
Show dot\-files in the file explorer.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-ascii\fR
Draw fold markers with ASCII characters (v and >).
.TP 7
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR, \fB\-\-no\-colour\fR
Draw without colours, as when NO_COLOR is set.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-no\-mouse\fR
Leave the mouse to the terminal rather than capture it.
.SS "Other options"
.TP 7
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a summary of the options (\-h), or this reference (\-\-help).
.TP 7
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version: aless 0.6.0.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-generate\fR \fIWHAT\fR
Print a file made from this reference: man, the man page (aless.1); or
complete\-bash, complete\-zsh, complete\-fish or complete\-powershell, that
shell\(aqs completions. See FILES.
.SH "INPUT AND FORMATS"
FILE is a path, or \- for standard input. Without a FILE, aless reads standard
input when it is not a terminal, and otherwise explores the current directory;
a directory named opens in the file explorer. Options end at \-\-, after which
every argument is a FILE, and a long option\(aqs value may follow it or be
attached to it (\-\-kind=yaml).
.PP
The format comes from a file\(aqs extension, or from its whole name for a
\-\-grammar NAME (/etc/hosts), and \-k FORMAT overrides it for every input;
inside aless, :format FORMAT reads a tab again as FORMAT, and :open PATH FORMAT
opens a file as one. A file whose extension no format claims is read as plain
text, an array of its lines. Map keys keep their source order. The formats,
with the extensions that imply each:
.TP 10
\fBjson\fR
json geojson har jsonld webmanifest
.TP 10
\fBjsonl\fR
jsonl ndjson
.TP 10
\fBjsonic\fR
jsonic
.TP 10
\fBjsonc\fR
jsonc (trailing commas accepted)
.TP 10
\fBjson5\fR
json5
.TP 10
\fByaml\fR
yaml yml
.TP 10
\fBtoml\fR
toml
.TP 10
\fBini\fR
ini cfg conf cnf
.TP 10
\fBcsv\fR
csv (records keyed by the header row)
.TP 10
\fBtsv\fR
tsv tab (as csv)
.TP 10
\fBxml\fR
xml svg xhtml xsd xsl xslt plist
.TP 10
\fBzon\fR
zon
.TP 10
\fBmarkdown\fR
md markdown (its syntax tree)
.TP 10
\fBfeed\fR
rss atom (normalised to an Atom shape)
.TP 10
\fBcss\fR
css (its syntax tree)
.TP 10
\fBproto\fR
proto (its FileDescriptorProto, as protoc writes one in JSON)
.TP 10
\fBpgn\fR
pgn (chess games, each its tags and moves)
.TP 10
\fBc\fR
c h (its concrete syntax tree, every token with its place)
.TP 10
\fBabnf\fR
abnf (the grammar spec its compiler writes, the three notations\(aq one schema)
.TP 10
\fBebnf\fR
ebnf (the grammar spec its compiler writes, the three notations\(aq one schema)
.TP 10
\fBgbnf\fR
gbnf (the grammar spec its compiler writes, the three notations\(aq one schema)
.TP 10
\fBexpr\fR
none, \-k expr (jsonic with arithmetic, an operation the list of its operator
and terms)
.TP 10
\fBsemver\fR
none, \-k semver (a version: its major, minor, patch, prerelease and build)
.TP 10
\fBtext\fR
txt text log (and every extension no format claims)
.SH "KEYS"
The keys are jless\(aqs, and F1 or :help inside aless lists them all; the ones
used most:
.TP 10
\fBj k\fR
down / up; a count before a key repeats it (3j)
.PD 0
.TP 10
\fBh l\fR
collapse / expand, or go to the parent / first child
.TP 10
\fBJ K\fR
next / previous sibling
.TP 10
\fBg G\fR
first / last row; Ng goes to row N
.TP 10
\fBC\-d C\-u\fR
half a page down / up
.TP 10
\fBSpace\fR
toggle the focused container
.TP 10
\fBc e\fR
collapse / expand the focused node and its siblings
.TP 10
\fB/ ?\fR
search forward / backward; n N for the next / previous match
.TP 10
\fByy yp yq\fR
copy the value / its path / its jq path
.TP 10
\fBm\fR
switch between data mode and line mode
.TP 10
\fBs\fR
show the source text at the focused node
.TP 10
\fB!\fR
show a parse error\(aqs report in full
.TP 10
\fBTab\fR
the next tab (Shift\-Tab the previous)
.TP 10
\fB:\fR
a command: :open PATH, :format FORMAT, :w FILE, :help
.TP 10
\fBq\fR
close the tab, quitting with the last; C\-c quits
.PD
.SH "CUSTOM GRAMMARS"
\-\-grammar NAME=FILE reads the files whose extension or whole name is NAME, in
any case, with the ABNF grammar in FILE (RFC 5234, compiled by tabnas/abnf),
and \-k NAME reads any input with it. ; @object a b and ; @array comments on a
rule say what it builds; without them the value is the compiler\(aqs parse
tree, a {"rule", "src", "kids"} node per rule. The input is read as plain text:
{ } [ ] : , are ordinary characters, # starts a comment to the end of the line,
and a word is the token TX (NR, ST and VL bring numbers, quoted strings and
true, false and null back). A grammar that does not compile is a usage error
before any input is read, and so is a repetition count over 1,024, or
repetitions that would have the compiler write more than 1,024 rules. An input
the grammar does not accept opens as a parse error, with the grammar\(aqs name
for its format.
.PP
Grammars for /etc/hosts, crontabs, /etc/passwd, /etc/group, /etc/fstab,
/etc/resolv.conf and KEY=value files, each with a sample and a guide to writing
one, are in aless\(aqs source:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
https://github.com/rjrodger/aless/tree/main/tests/fixtures/grammars
.fi
.RE
.SH "LARGE INPUTS AND LIMITS"
An input is read whole and parsed whole before anything is shown, at about a
megabyte a second and 40 bytes of memory per byte of input. The parse holds the
viewer: before a large file is read aless says so, and a reload of a large
watched file pauses it for as long as the parse takes. \-\-max\-size (default
64M) refuses a larger input before it is read, as a tab that says so and names
the size that would read it, and \-\-timeout stops a parse that runs too long,
as a tab that says how far it got.
.PP
A document nested deeper than aless reads opens as a parse error with the code
too_deep: past about 1,000 levels, or sooner where the grammar has a limit of
its own (127 levels for JSON, JSONL, JSONic, JSON5, YAML, TOML, INI, ZON and
expressions, an expression of 127 operations too, 256 for XML, 512 for JSONC,
768 open rules for CSS, braces 100 deep for a .proto file, counted before it is
parsed, for C an expression of 127 operations, 1,024 open rules and a syntax
tree 256 levels deep, or 256 values a byte of the file written out (33,554,432
at most) or 2,048 bytes of names and strings a byte (256 MB at most), its
reader repeating subtrees, and for a grammar its compiler\(aqs, 2,048 open
rules for ABNF, 520 for EBNF and 512 for GBNF, some 500, 130 and 128 nested
groups, and elements nested 128 deep).
.PP
Numbers are 64\-bit floats: the tree, :w and the copy commands give an integer
beyond 2\(ha53 as the nearest one, and the source view (s) has the spelling.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP 7
\fB0\fR
the viewer was quit
.TP 7
\fB1\fR
the viewer failed while it ran: the terminal could not be drawn on or read
.TP 7
\fB2\fR
the viewer could not start: bad usage (an unknown option, a bad value, a
\-\-grammar that does not compile or cannot be read), or no terminal to draw on
and read keys from
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP 10
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
set and not empty: no colours, as \-\-no\-color
.TP 10
\fBTERM\fR
dumb: a terminal that cannot draw the viewer, which refuses to start, as
without one
.SH "FILES"
A release archive carries the man page and the completions, which \-\-generate
also prints:
.TP 18
\fBman/aless.1\fR
the man page (\-\-generate man)
.PD 0
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/aless.bash\fR
bash (\-\-generate complete\-bash)
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/_aless\fR
zsh (\-\-generate complete\-zsh)
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/aless.fish\fR
fish (\-\-generate complete\-fish)
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/_aless.ps1\fR
PowerShell (\-\-generate complete\-powershell)
.PD
.PP
A shell reads its completions from where it looks for them (a package puts them
there), or from its startup file each time it starts:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
eval "$(aless \-\-generate complete\-bash)"     # \(ti/.bashrc
eval "$(aless \-\-generate complete\-zsh)"      # \(ti/.zshrc, after compinit
aless \-\-generate complete\-fish | source      # config.fish
.fi
.RE
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.nf
aless config.yaml                      one file, in a tab
aless deploy.yaml values.toml a.csv    several files, a tab each
curl \-s https://api.example/x | aless  standard input, read as JSON
aless \-k yaml settings.conf            a file whose extension misleads
aless \-\-grammar hosts=hosts.abnf /etc/hosts    a format of your own
aless \-\-no\-watch big.json              no reload when the file changes
aless \-m line \-n x.json                line mode, with line numbers
aless                                  explore the current directory
.fi
